---
// Sources and licences of the Old English Reader (src/pages/sources.astro).
import { FIXES, SPELLINGS, WITHOUT_ENTRY } from "../../packs/ang/build/lemmas";
import { CHAPTER_STARTS, MISSING_VERSES } from "./alignment";
import { ISWOC_COMMIT, SOURCES, THORPE_REVISIONS } from "./sources";

const pins = Object.entries(SOURCES).map(([file, [url, sha]]) => ({ file, url, sha }));
const pages = Object.keys(THORPE_REVISIONS).map(Number);
---
<h2>Old English</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>ISWOC, the Oslo Old English treebank (Kristin Bech and Kristine Eide, 2014)</dt>
  <dd>
    Every word’s form, lemma, part of speech, morphology and punctuation come from ISWOC’s two
    files <code>apt.xml</code> (<i>Apollonius of Tyre</i>) and <code>wscp.xml</code> (the West-Saxon Gospels), in PROIEL XML,
    as <a href="https://syntacticus.org/">Syntacticus</a> publishes them
    (<a href="https://github.com/syntacticus/syntacticus-treebank-data">syntacticus-treebank-data</a>,
    commit <code>{ISWOC_COMMIT.slice(0, 12)}</code>). Both files are
    marked <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>, so this reader’s Old English text
    and annotation, and the word data built from them, may be reused only for non-commercial purposes, with attribution
    and under the same licence. Every sentence in the two files is marked reviewed. The reader shows each token’s form
    as the treebank prints it, with its punctuation; <i>næs</i>, <i>to gegaderod</i> and the like, which ISWOC writes
    as two tokens joined, are one written word with two analyses, and forms ISWOC prints with a space
    (<i>æt nehstan</i>) stay one word.
  </dd>
  <dt>Apollonius of Tyre: Peter Goolden’s edition (1958)</dt>
  <dd>
    ISWOC took the text from the York–Toronto–Helsinki Corpus of Old English Prose, whose <i>coapollo</i> is Goolden’s
    edition, <i>The Old English Apollonius of Tyre</i> (Oxford University Press, 1958), of Cambridge, Corpus Christi
    College 201. That edition is in copyright. The reader uses it only as ISWOC redistributes it, under the
    non-commercial, share-alike licence above, which is the only grant this reader relies on for it. The Old English is
    the first part of the story, the treebank’s title sentence and 22 chapters; the part after the break in the
    manuscript is not in the treebank.
  </dd>
  <dt>The West-Saxon Gospel of Mark: Walter W. Skeat’s edition (1871–77)</dt>
  <dd>
    ISWOC’s text of Mark is Skeat’s edition of Corpus Christi College 140, which is in the public domain. All sixteen
    chapters are present, but the Old English has no verse {MISSING_VERSES.join(", ")} (they are not in the edition);
    the reader shows the King James verse alone for those seven rows.
  </dd>
  <dt>Morphology</dt>
  <dd>
    ISWOC’s PROIEL tags are converted to Universal Dependencies (<code>packs/ang/build/morph.ts</code>; the build
    stops on any tag or value the conversion does not know). Nouns, adjectives, participles and pronouns keep their
    case, gender and number; verbs their person, number, tense and mood. Auxiliaries
    are <i>wesan</i>, <i>bēon</i>, the modals (
    <i>magan</i>, <i>willan</i>, <i>sculan</i>, <i>mōtan</i>, <i>durran</i>, <i>þurfan</i>)
    and <i>habban</i> when ISWOC tags it auxiliary. A demonstrative or quantifier that modifies a noun
    is a determiner, otherwise a pronoun. Where ISWOC marks a value as undecided, the reader says so rather than choosing:
    case <i>e</i> (accusative or dative), <i>c</i> (genitive or dative) and <i>o</i> (any case but the nominative)
    become several values (<i>accusative or dative</i>), and the same for gender (<i>p</i>, <i>o</i>, <i>r</i>); an
    uncertain value (<i>x</i>) and a value that says nothing are left out.
  </dd>
  <dt>Lemmas</dt>
  <dd>
    ISWOC’s lemmas are ASCII, spell <i>and</i> as <code>&amp;</code> and number homographs. The reader finds each
    lemma’s Bosworth–Toller entry (ignoring length marks, case, ð and þ, hyphens), and shows Bosworth–Toller’s
    headword with its length marks as macrons (<i>hālig</i>). A lemma ISWOC spells differently from the dictionary
    keeps ISWOC’s spelling, and its entry is “listed as” the dictionary’s ({Object.keys(SPELLINGS).length} lemmas). ISWOC lemmatizes
    some words under an inflected form, a misspelling or one of several variants; {Object.keys(FIXES).length} lemmas are
    corrected by hand, among them the nine lemmas of the third-person pronoun (
    <i>his</i>, <i>him</i>, <i>hit</i>, <i>hī</i>, <i>hēo</i> …), which count together
    under <i>hē</i>, and the variant lemmas of one name
    (<i>Satanas</i> and <i>Satan</i>). {Object.keys(WITHOUT_ENTRY).length} lemmas have no entry in Bosworth–Toller and take
    Wiktionary’s or none. The build stops on a lemma that neither dictionary has and that is not listed
    (<code>packs/ang/build/lemmas.ts</code>), and on a listed lemma that nothing uses.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>English</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Benjamin Thorpe, <i>The Anglo-Saxon Version of the Story of Apollonius of Tyre</i> (1834)</dt>
  <dd>
    Thorpe’s literal English translation, which faces his Old English text, is in the public domain. The reader
    uses <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Index:The_Anglo-Saxon_version_of_the_story_of_Apollonius_of_Tyre.djvu">Wikisource’s
    proofread transcription</a> of pages {pages[0]}–{pages.at(-1)}, each page pinned to the revision named
    in <code>corpora/oldenglish/sources.ts</code> and fetched through the MediaWiki API. Wikisource’s text and transcription
    are available under <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>. The page numbers of
    the Old English printed in brackets are removed, words split over a page break are joined, and seven misreadings of
    the scan (<i>hut</i>, <i>hack</i>, <i>und</i> twice, <i>lie</i>, <i>hare</i>, <i>end</i>) are corrected; the build
    stops if one of them is gone or occurs twice. Thorpe’s footnotes are kept (the <i>n</i> buttons).
  </dd>
  <dt>Cutting Thorpe’s English to ISWOC’s chapters</dt>
  <dd>
    Thorpe does not divide his text into chapters. The chapter of each row is the stretch of his English from the clause
    that translates the chapter’s first Old English words to the one that translates the next chapter’s. The
    {CHAPTER_STARTS.length} opening phrases were found by hand (<code>corpora/oldenglish/alignment.ts</code>); the
    build stops unless each occurs once and in order. Three chapters begin in the middle of an English sentence (the
    previous row then ends with a comma). Where the Old English breaks off, Thorpe goes on with a translation of the
    Latin, and he notes that the Old English resumes later; the reader stops where ISWOC’s text stops, with his note.
    The alignment is a judgement, not data.
  </dd>
  <dt>The King James Version of Mark (1611)</dt>
  <dd>
    Public domain, taken from <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/ebooks/10">Project Gutenberg’s ebook #10</a>, one verse
    to a row. It translates the Greek, not the Latin Vulgate that the West-Saxon version follows, so the two differ
    in wording and sometimes in content. Project Gutenberg re-releases its ebooks now and then; the build checks the
    file’s hash.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>Dictionaries</h2>
<dl>
  <dt>Bosworth–Toller, <i>An Anglo-Saxon Dictionary Online</i> (Ondřej Tichý et al., Charles University)</dt>
  <dd>
    Joseph Bosworth and T. Northcote Toller’s dictionary (1898), with Toller’s Supplement (1921), as digitized and
    released by <a href="http://hdl.handle.net/11234/1-3532">LINDAT/CLARIAH-CZ</a> (data of 2021-04-09, version 0.1),
    licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>. The reader turns each entry’s XML
    into numbered senses with a few Old English citations and their English, and marks long vowels with macrons where
    the dictionary marks them with acutes. A cross-reference entry (<i>v. þanne</i>) opens its target’s. The dictionary’s
    own readme warns that not all of its entries have been checked or tagged.
  </dd>
  <dt>Wiktionary, Old English entries</dt>
  <dd>
    For words Bosworth–Toller lacks, the entries of <a href="https://en.wiktionary.org/">English Wiktionary</a>
    as <a href="https://kaikki.org/">kaikki.org</a> extracts them,
    licensed <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/">CC BY-SA 4.0</a> (and GFDL). kaikki.org regenerates its
    file from Wiktionary’s dumps, so upstream will drift: the build checks the file against the extract this reader was
    made with.
  </dd>
  <dt>Names</dt>
  <dd>
    The line that says who or what each proper name is was written for this reader; neither dictionary covers most of
    them.
  </dd>
</dl>

<h2>Pinned files</h2>
<p>
  The build downloads these files into <code>.cache/sources/oldenglish</code> and stops if a file’s SHA-256 is not the
  one below.
</p>
<table class="pins">
  <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>SHA-256</th></tr></thead>
  <tbody>
    {pins.map(({ file, url, sha }) => <tr><td><a href={url}><code>{file}</code></a></td><td><code>{sha}</code></td></tr>)}
  </tbody>
</table>

<style>
  .pins {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
  }
  .pins th, .pins td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 0.2rem 0.8rem 0.2rem 0;
    vertical-align: top;
  }
  .pins code {
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
